ENTRY            C2766   20260807   20261014   20261014       C259
SUBENT        C2766001   20260807   20261014   20261014       C259
BIB                 10         38
TITLE      Constraining the 85Rb branching point in the
           astrophysical gamma process
AUTHOR     (K.Bosmpotinis,A.Spyrou,A.Tsantiri,H.C.Berg,
           P.A.DeYoung,A.C.Dombos,P.Giuliani,S.N.Liddick,
           S.M.Lyons,P.Mohr,O.Olivas-Gomez,A.Palmisano-Kyle,
           J.Pereira,A.L.Richard,A.Simon,R.G.T.Zegers)
REFERENCE  (J,PR/C,114,015801,2026)
           #doi:10.1103/lqw3-gdvb
INSTITUTE  (1USAMSU,1USAHPE,1USABNW,3HUNDEB,1USAJHU,1USATEN,
           1USAOHO,1USANOT)
FACILITY   (LINAC,1USAMSU) The ReAccelerator (ReA) facility at
           the National Superconducting Cyclotron Laboratory,
           Michigan State University.
DETECTOR   (NAICR) SuN is a 4pi calorimeter, 16 inch in diameter
           and 16 inch long, that consists of two semicylinders
           to detect gamma rays with high efficiency. Each
           semicylinder is segmented into four optically
           isolated NaI(Tl) crystals, with three photomultipliers
            (PMTs) attached to each crystal. SuN has a borehole
           along its axis that is 1.8 inch in diameter. The
           large angular coverage and the high detection
           efficiency make SuN ideal for the application of the
           gamma-summing technique.
SAMPLE     The target consisted of a 4-cm-long gas cell placed
           at the geometrical center of the SuN detector. The
           gas cell had a 2-microm-thick Mo foil on the front and
           back to contain the hydrogen gas. The pressure inside
           the gas cell was kept at ~600 Torr.
METHOD     The experiment was conducted in inverse kinematics
           where a stable beam of 84Kr27+ was accelerated at
           energies 2.8, 3.1, 3.4, and 3.7 MeV/u and impinged
           onto a gas target of hydrogen. The gamma-rays from
           the nuclear reaction 84Kr+p -> 85Rb* originating
           from the deexcitation of the compound nucleus were
           registered by the Summing NaI(Tl) (SuN) detector.
CORRECTION To correct for the Doppler peak broadening,
           Doppler-shift corrections were applied on each segment.
HISTORY    (20260807C) BP
ENDBIB              38
NOCOMMON             0          0
ENDSUBENT           41
SUBENT        C2766002   20260807   20261014   20261014       C259
BIB                  4         16
REACTION   (36-KR-84(P,G)37-RB-85,,SIG)
ANALYSIS   The experimental reaction cross section can be
           extracted as: sigma=(Y*A)/(Na*Nt*Nb*epsilon), where
           Y is the total number of reactions detected, A is the
           target mass, Na is the Avogadro number, Nt is the
           target thickness, Nb is the number of particles
           impinging onto the gas cell, and epsilon is the
           efficiency of SuN.
ERR-ANALYS (ERR-T) Total uncertainty.
           (ERR-S,1.,4.) Statistical uncertainty.
           (ERR-1) The efficiency of SuN.
           (ERR-2) The beam current measurements.
           (ERR-3) The gas cell pressure.
           (ERR-4,4.,6.) Variations in the cross section due to
           simulation and the use of different models.
STATUS     (TABLE,,K.Bosmpotinis+,J,PR/C,114,015801,2026) Table I
